WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.044
[synth music]

00:00:04.462 --> 00:00:08.174 align:start position:0% line:0%
This was a pop up exhibit
for first-year physics students,

00:00:08.174 --> 00:00:09.384 align:start position:0% line:0%
and it's all about

00:00:09.384 --> 00:00:11.845 align:start position:0% line:0%
introducing them to rare books
that are related

00:00:11.845 --> 00:00:13.471
to their first year
physics classes.

00:00:13.471 --> 00:00:14.556
And that means
that these are

00:00:14.556 --> 00:00:17.851
the oldest and rarest
books at the Beinecke.

00:00:17.851 --> 00:00:21.604 align:start position:0%
(Chiara)
Oh, these are all of
the calculations!

00:00:21.604 --> 00:00:23.898
To be able to share that
with my students

00:00:23.898 --> 00:00:25.942
and to see how excited
they all were,

00:00:25.942 --> 00:00:28.069
undergraduate students,
graduate students,

00:00:28.069 --> 00:00:29.696
it was just such a treat.

00:00:30.321 --> 00:00:31.448
I think that
these books are

00:00:31.448 --> 00:00:34.826
so important, even in
2023 for a few reasons.

00:00:34.826 --> 00:00:36.494
Number one,
a lot of the things that

00:00:36.494 --> 00:00:38.079
they wrote
down are still true today.

00:00:38.079 --> 00:00:40.165
And that's the amazing thing
about science.

00:00:40.415 --> 00:00:42.333 align:start position:0%
(student #1)
Currently, inventing calculus.

00:00:43.334 --> 00:00:43.960 align:start position:0%
(student #1)
Super cool.

00:00:44.043 --> 00:00:46.004
The second thing is
that some of these books

00:00:46.004 --> 00:00:48.465
go into great detail
about how difficult

00:00:48.465 --> 00:00:50.133
these calculations were.

00:00:50.133 --> 00:00:52.844
And those kind of feelings
are the things that we

00:00:52.844 --> 00:00:55.638
also feel as researchers
and scientists.

00:00:55.638 --> 00:00:57.682
And so giving students
that connection

00:00:57.682 --> 00:00:59.851
it's just such an amazing link
to the past

00:00:59.851 --> 00:01:01.770
and makes these books
really relevant,

00:01:01.770 --> 00:01:03.772
not only from the historical
point of view,

00:01:03.772 --> 00:01:05.899
but from a cultural, scientific
point of view.

00:01:05.899 --> 00:01:07.984
These are their academic
ancestors.

00:01:07.984 --> 00:01:08.860
For me, that's the most

00:01:08.860 --> 00:01:10.153
important thing, is saying that

00:01:10.153 --> 00:01:12.989
these really famous scientists
that are elevated

00:01:12.989 --> 00:01:15.492
were clearly brilliant people,
but they're still just people.

00:01:15.492 --> 00:01:18.495
And the books themselves
have such interesting character.

00:01:18.578 --> 00:01:21.122
There's other people
that wrote in the margins

00:01:21.122 --> 00:01:23.416
and really just the sense
of holding a piece of history

00:01:23.416 --> 00:01:25.460
piece of history
in your hand.

00:01:25.460 --> 00:01:27.170
It was really incredible.

